"Far from city lights, on a clear summer night, a pale, glowing band stretches across the sky like a river of mist. In Chinese, it is called the Silver River or the River of Heaven; in English, it is the Milky Way. For thousands of years, people wondered what it was. Was it a cloud? A road across the sky? A river of milk?": "7fb140d52943",
"Far from city lights, on a clear summer night, a pale, glowing band stretches across the sky like a river of mist. In Chinese, it is called the Silver River or the River of Heaven; in English, it is the Milky Way. For thousands of years, people wondered what it was. Was it a cloud? A road across the sky? A river of milk? In 1610, Galileo turned one of the first telescopes toward it and saw that the band is made of countless faint stars, too far away and too close together for our eyes to tell apart. Today we know that the Milky Way is our galaxy: a huge, flat disk of hundreds of billions of stars, along with gas and dust, all held together by gravity. Our Sun is just one of those stars. So why does it look like a band? We live inside the disk, about 26,000 light-years from its center. When we look along the disk, our view passes through enormous numbers of stars, and their light blends into a glowing band. When we look up or down, out of the disk, we see far fewer. Imagine standing in a crowd spread out across a wide, flat field: look straight ahead and you see people all around you, but look up and you see only sky. The disk is about 100,000 light-years across but only about 1,000 light-years thick, flatter than a pancake. At its center is a bulge of older stars and a black hole about four million times as heavy as the Sun. Spiral arms wind out from the center, and the whole galaxy turns. The Sun takes about 230 million years to go around once. The last time it was where it is now, the first dinosaurs were appearing. In Chinese stories, the Milky Way is the river that keeps the Cowherd and the Weaver Girl apart. Look for them in the evening sky: Altair and Vega shine on opposite banks of the band. The ancient Book of Songs describes the bright River of Clouds turning across the sky. The Greeks said it was milk spilled by the goddess Hera, and that is why our word galaxy comes from the Greek word for milk. Sadly, more than a third of the people on Earth can no longer see the Milky Way from home, because city lights hide it. In Taiwan, the best time to see its bright center is a summer evening, from a dark place such as the Hehuan Mountain Dark Sky Park. Use the chart below to find where the band lies over Changhua tonight.": "4b274cae21b9",
